{"verdict":"true","basis":"confirmed","reason":"In src/jira.ts:4, getIssue calls `fetch(`${JIRA}/issue/${key}`)` with no options at all: no `signal`, no AbortSignal.timeout, and no AbortController. Its only caller, the `issue` route handler in src/routes.ts:48, awaits it directly and adds no timeout either. The repository sets no global dispatcher or agent timeout anywhere (a grep for timeout, AbortSignal, setGlobalDispatcher and undici finds nothing), and package.json has no HTTP client dependency. So the only limits are Node's built-in undici defaults: about 10s to connect, and about 300s each for headers and for body idle time. A Jira server that accepts the connection but responds slowly or stalls keeps each issue request pending for up to roughly five minutes, possibly longer if it trickles the body. `res.json()` has no bound either. The review's wording "hangs every caller" is a little too strong, because the wait is bounded by undici's defaults and does not block the event loop. The main defect is real, though: the app sets no timeout of its own. searchAssigned (line 10) has the same pattern.","evidence":[{"location":"src/jira.ts:3","code":"export async function getIssue(key: string): Promise<unknown> {
